<!-- Exercise 064 - KeyFrameAnimation (intermediate).
     Goal:   Declare a keyframe Animation inside a Style and let it take over one
     named Border's Opacity, leaving the sibling Border alone. Where ex063 used a
     Transition, which reacts to a value CHANGING, this one runs unprompted from
     the moment the style attaches.
     Drills: Style.Animations, Animation with Duration, IterationCount and
     PlaybackDirection, KeyFrame with Cue, and animation Setters.
     Passes: dotnet test, filtered to Ex064_ (see Ex064_KeyFrameAnimation.axaml.cs
     for the exact command, because an XML comment may not hold two hyphens in a
     row).

     WHAT THIS EXERCISE DOES NOT ASSERT, AND WHY: how far the animation has got.
     Nothing pumps the animation clock in a headless test, and nothing public can
     be made to - IClock, ClockBase and Animatable.Clock are all internal in
     Avalonia 12.1.1, and forcing render-timer ticks advances frames, not time.
     What IS deterministic, and measured: the instant the style attaches, the
     animation takes ownership of Opacity at BindingPriority.Animation and holds a
     value inside the range its keyframes declare. That is a real attachment
     proof, it needs no clock, and it is what the test checks. An honest gap here
     beats a wall-clock race. -->
<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             x:Class="FeWoLearning.Avalonia.Exercises.Intermediate.Ex064_KeyFrameAnimation">
  <!-- TODO: replace the placeholder below with two Borders and a Style whose
       Animations run on the first of them only:

         <StackPanel Spacing="8">
           <StackPanel.Styles>
             <Style Selector="Border#Pulser">
               <Style.Animations>
                 <Animation Duration="0:0:5"
                            IterationCount="Infinite"
                            PlaybackDirection="Alternate">
                   <KeyFrame Cue="0%">
                     <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="1.0" />
                   </KeyFrame>
                   <KeyFrame Cue="100%">
                     <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.2" />
                   </KeyFrame>
                 </Animation>
               </Style.Animations>
             </Style>
           </StackPanel.Styles>
           <Border Name="Pulser" Width="40" Height="40" Background="#FF3366" />
           <Border Name="Still" Width="40" Height="40" Background="#CCCCCC" />
         </StackPanel>

       IterationCount="Infinite" is not decoration: a finite animation would run
       out and hand Opacity back, so ownership would depend on when the test
       looked. Infinite makes the attachment permanent and therefore assertable.
       Still must be left out of the selector - the test asserts that its Opacity
       is NOT owned by an animation, which a selector of plain "Border" would
       break. -->
  <StackPanel>
    <TextBlock Text="TODO" />
  </StackPanel>
</UserControl>
